/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a straight column of length L, area A and second moment of area I.
   - E is Young's modulus in Pa; I is the second moment of area about the bending axis in m⁴; v is the lateral deflection in m; x is distance along the column in m; P is the axial compression in N.
   - The formula is Euler's critical load.
   - End fixity decides how much of the column can bend.
   - Pcr is the Euler buckling load in N; K is the effective-length factor; L is the real column length in m.
   - Fixed-free is the weakest because the free tip can move sideways with no support.
   - Slenderness measures how thin a column is.
   - λ is the slenderness ratio; Le is the effective length in m; r is the radius of gyration in m; I is the second moment of area in m⁴; A is the area in m².
   - Euler's formula is valid while the buckling stress Pcr/A is below the proportional limit.
   - A column buckles about the axis with the smallest I.
*/
