/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A short column fails by crushing at the squash load Pc = σc A.
   - Rankine added the reciprocals: 1/PR = 1/Pc + 1/PE.
   - PR is the Rankine-Gordon failure load in N; σc the crushing stress in N/mm²; A the area in mm²; λ = Le/r the slenderness ratio; r = √(I/A) the radius of gyration in mm; a the Rankine constant, dimensionless when σc and E share one unit.
   - The derivation is two lines.
   - At λ = 0 the term aλ² vanishes and PR = σcA = Pc.
   - PR never exceeds either load.
   - σR is the average compressive stress at failure in N/mm²; compare it with the allowable stress, not with σc alone.
   - The constant a normally comes from tests or a handbook, because real struts carry initial bow, residual stress and end restraint that the ideal derivation ignores; a value from σc/(π²E) is only a first estimate. λ must always use Le for the
*/
